2.Using @ConfigurationProperties (Recommended)

Best for structured and dynamic configs.

ex  application.properties
app.name=MySpringApp
app.timeout=5000
app.server.host=localhost
app.server.port=8080


in class
@Component
@ConfigurationProperties(prefix = "app")
public class AppConfig {

    private String name;
    private int timeout;
    private Server server;

}
Why @ConfigurationProperties is Better Than @Value
| Feature                  | `@Value` | `@ConfigurationProperties` |
| ------------------------ | -------- | -------------------------- |
| Supports properties file | ✅        | ✅                          |
| Supports YAML            | ✅        | ✅                          |
| Type-safe                | ❌        | ✅                          |
| Supports nested objects  | ❌        | ✅                          |
| Easy to maintain         | ❌        | ✅                          |

type safe means 
Type checking at runtime: Spring ensures that the value in the configuration matches the type of the field.
If you accidentally write timeout: "abc" (a string) instead of a number, Spring will fail to start and give an error.

No manual parsing needed: You don’t have to manually convert strings to integers, booleans, lists, etc. Spring does it for you automatically.

*
diff between properties and yml file  , in what scenario which to use

Both application.yml and application.properties are used for configuration in Spring Boot.
They do the same job, but they differ in syntax, readability, and use cases.


| Aspect         | `application.properties`          | `application.yml`        |
| -------------- | --------------------------------- | ------------------------ |
| Format         | Key = Value                       | YAML (indentation-based) |
| Readability    | Less readable for complex configs | More readable & clean    |
| Structure      | Flat (dot notation)               | Hierarchical             |
| Indentation    | Not required                      | **Required**             |
| Risk of errors | Low                               | Higher (wrong spaces)    |
| Learning curve | Very easy                         | Moderate                 |


Profiles Support (Important Difference)
Properties (multiple files)
application-dev.properties
application-prod.properties

YAML (single file)
spring:
  profiles:
    active: dev

---
spring:
  config:
    activate:
      on-profile: dev
server:
  port: 8081

---
spring:
  config:
    activate:
      on-profile: prod
server:
  port: 8080

👉
YAML allows multiple profiles in one file, which is very powerful.

👉 Use application.yml when:
You have nested configs
You use @ConfigurationProperties
You want clean, readable configs

👉 Use application.properties when:
Config is very small
You want simplicity
Team is more comfortable with it



*
if configure the same values in both yml and properties file then ? and who will load first
-> properties file will load 1st
If the same configuration is present in both application.yml and application.properties, 
Spring Boot loads both but gives higher priority to application.properties, so its value overrides the YAML value.

